coolest since 2022.","t_yest":"November 13: cooler than 81% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>45\u00b0F</strong> was the 25th coolest November 13 high of 130 since 1897, the coolest since 2022. The normal high for the date is 56\u00b0F, and the record is 82\u00b0F (1955). The low of <strong>36\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Evansville has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Evansville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Evansville has set <strong class=\"sig\">28 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong>88 record highs</strong>, close to the 75 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (50). Evansville's all-time high is 111\u00b0F, on July 28, 1930.","t_chg":"Evansville's nights cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986. Its days warmed over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 129 complete years: Evansville's average low has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.16\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.24 to \u22120.08), about \u22122.1\u00b0F since 1897. Evansville's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.13\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.07 to +0.20), about +1.7\u00b0F since 1897. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.60\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.28 to +0.93) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 5 months fell. Highs rose in 4 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Evansville has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Evansville's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.76 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.39 to +1.13), about +9.7 inches since 1897. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 47.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2011 (70.03 in); the driest, 1930 (25.55 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}